\section{A safety margin on a chance constraint}{

The probability in a chance constraint is estimated from the scenarios, so
it carries sampling error. For a target \code{p} and \code{n} scenarios, its standard
error is \code{sqrt(p * (1 - p) / n)}, about 0.013 for \code{p = 0.9} and
\code{n = 512}. A solution chosen to just meet the target on its own scenarios
therefore misses the target on new scenarios about half the time (see
\code{\link[=resample]{resample()}}).

\code{margin = k} raises the target by \code{k} standard errors, so that the true
probability meets the original target with a confidence of about
\code{pnorm(k)}: 84\% for \code{k = 1}, 98\% for \code{k = 2}. For an upper limit, such as
\code{prob(...) <= 0.1}, the target is lowered instead. A margin applies only
when one side of the constraint is a \code{\link[=prob]{prob()}} and the other a number
strictly between 0 and 1. The standard error is computed from the number
of scenarios when the model is solved, so the margin can be given before
\code{\link[=set_scenarios]{set_scenarios()}} is called.
}

\section{A constraint with an on-off switch}{

\code{when = b}, with \code{b} a binary variable from \code{\link[=bin_var]{bin_var()}}, makes the
constraint apply only in solutions in which \code{b} is 1. For example,
\code{add(m, output <= 0, when = closed)} forces the output to 0 only if the
plant is closed. \code{b} is a single variable, or a vector variable with one
element per constraint.

In a model without random variables, a switch limits the kind of model
the service accepts: every variable must then be an integer or a binary
variable, with finite bounds. The same holds for \code{\link[=holds]{holds()}}, \code{max()} and
\code{min()}.
}

